WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:04.170
Rhiannon: The Care Programme Approach
is a package of care that may be

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:07.910
used to plan mental healthcare and
is there to support young people’s

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:09.500
recovery from mental illness.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:14.470
It is a framework used to assess young
people’s needs, and make sure that

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:18.290
they have the correct support for
those needs from specialist teams of

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:22.340
mental health professionals such as
mental health nurses and psychiatrists.

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:29.009
Leah: Different mental health
services will have policies about

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:34.039
who is able to get help under CPA
and a care coordinator will monitor

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:38.430
that care and support with regular
reviews, to see if needs have changed

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:40.460
and a crisis plan must be included.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.200
A CPA aims to support mental health
recovery by helping an individual to

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:49.370
understand their strengths, goals,
support needs, and difficulties.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.789
It should be available if a child
or young person has a wide range

00:00:52.799 --> 00:00:56.440
of needs from different services
or are thought to be a high risk.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:01.970
A child or young person may get help or
be offered support under a CPA if they

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:07.759
have a severe mental disorder, are at risk
of suicide, self-harm or harm to others.

00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:13.019
They may also be eligible if they neglect
themselves, are vulnerable due to various

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.330
reasons or misuse drugs and alcohol.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:19.540
Rhiannon: There are other things that
may qualify them such as needing the

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:24.610
support of a carer or having their own
caring responsibilities or having been

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:26.619
detained under the mental health act.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.179
They may also get help if they have
recently been put in touch with a

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.380
crisis team, have physical health
problems or disability, housing

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.040
problems or ethnicity issues.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:40.199
Local teams will have their own
policies, but these will be closely

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.920
linked to the general guidelines.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.899
Leah: Young people who have been
detained under the Mental Health Act

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:49.660
will be supported through the CPA after
they are discharged from hospital.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:54.100
A care plan is drawn up and this a
written plan of how their care needs

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:58.419
will be met including information about
treatment and any other areas of their

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:03.110
life that may need supporting, such
as housing, education or employment.

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:11.080
Rhiannon: Other things likely to be
considered are personal circumstances,

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:17.049
medication, therapy, physical health
needs, problems with drugs and alcohol and

00:02:17.059 --> 00:02:19.850
any risks of harm to themselves or others.

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:24.830
A multi-disciplinary approach will
be used to assess needs and make the

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:28.890
care plan and a copy of this plan
will be available for the individual

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.040
or a parent or carer if they wish.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:35.920
It will also have full details of
what to do in a crisis and once a

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.870
young person turns 17 and they need
to move to adult services this will

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:44.950
be planned as part of their care plan.

